.TH FRAMECLOCK 2
.SH NAME
frameclock: ticker, tickms, rate, report \- a clock that keeps a frame rate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "frameclock.m";
fclock := load Frameclock Frameclock->PATH;

fclock->init();

ticks := chan of int;
spawn fclock->ticker(ticks, 60);
spawn fclock->tickms(ticks, 250);

(frames, us) := fclock->rate();
fclock->report("mygame");
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Frameclock
sends a tick on
.I c
at
.I hz
frames a second, or every
.I ms
milliseconds, and keeps to that rate.
The value sent is the number of milliseconds since the previous tick, so
a caller whose simulation has a timestep of its own can run the right
number of substeps for the time that actually passed; a caller that
ignores it sees the
.B "chan of int"
it always had.
.PP
Keeping to a rate is not the same as sleeping for a length of time, and
this is the whole reason the module exists.
Sleeping adds the time a frame's work takes to the sleep instead of
taking it out, so the period is always longer than asked and grows with
the work.
And on this host the sleep overshoots on its own account: measured,
.I nanosleep
of 16ms takes 18.7ms, a fifth over, and it is a fifth over at every
length from 1ms to 33ms.
A port that did nothing at all therefore got 53 frames a second when it
asked for 60.
.PP
The fix is not a better sleep but an absolute deadline: ask for the time
between now and when the next frame is due, and the overshoot comes off
the following ask rather than adding to the period.
With the very same
.I nanosleep
that gives 18.70ms a frame when asked for 16, this gives 16.699ms.
.PP
If a frame's work runs past the next deadline the frames that were missed
are dropped and the clock starts again from now, so a port that stalls
for a second does not then get a second's worth of ticks in a burst.
.PP
.B rate
returns the frames delivered and the mean period in microseconds.
A tick is not delivered until the consumer takes it, so this is the rate
the caller actually drew at and not the rate it asked for.
.B report
prints that.
Neither needs anything of the caller: when
.B /env/INFERNO_FPS
is set the ticker reports itself every two seconds, and when it is not it
says nothing at all.
.SH EXAMPLE
Every animated
.IR temple (1)
port is on this clock.
To see what one of them achieves:
.EX
; bind -a '#e' /env
; echo -n 1 > /env/INFERNO_FPS
; temple/psalmody
frameclock: 59.99 fps over 360 frames, 16.669 ms a frame (asked 16.666)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/frameclock.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR sys-sleep (2)
.SH BUGS
The mean period is honest and the jitter is not reported.
A frame that arrives 4ms late and one 4ms early average to the right
answer and do not look the same.
.PP
A consumer slower than the frame rate simply gets fewer frames \(em the
tick is not delivered until it is taken \(em so
.B rate
falls rather than the clock running ahead.
That is the intent, but it means a low number does not say whether the
clock or the caller is the slow one.
.PP
.I temple/god
is not on this clock.
Its ticker sends without blocking and stops when a popup closes, and this
module does neither.
